## DESCRIPTION
## Algebra, Exponential functions
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Exponential and logarithmic expressions and functions)
## Date(06/16/2017)
## Institution(Colorado Community College System)
## Author(James Morski, Hint added Brittni Lorton March 2020)
## Static(1)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('algebra','exponential functions')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

@x = (1 .. 4);
$a = non_zero_random(-40,  40,  10);
$b = non_zero_random(-100, 100, 50);
$f = Compute("$a x + $b")->reduce;
@y = map { $f->eval(x => $_) } @x;

$popup = DropDown([ 'linear', 'exponential', 'neither' ], 'linear');

BEGIN_PGML
Determine whether the table could represent a function that is linear, exponential, or neither.  If the function is exponential or linear, find a function that passes through the points. (If exponential, write the form [`f(x)=ab^x`], if linear, write the form [`f(x)=mx+b`])

[#
    [.[`x`].] [.[$x[0]].][.[$x[1]].][.[$x[2]].][.[$x[3]].]*
    [.[`f(x)`].] [.[$y[0]].][.[$y[1]].][.[$y[2]].][.[$y[3]].]
#]{horizontalrules =>1, align => '|c|c|c|c|c|', padding => 0.3}


The function is [_]{$popup} 

An form of the function is [` f(x)= `] [_]{$f}{20} 

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Look at how the values of [`f(x)`] are changing, are they changing by the same amount each time such as adding 5 each time? If so, that would be linear.

Or are they changing by the same factor each time such as cutting in half each time? If so, that would be exponential.
END_PGML_HINT

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Since each change in the [`x`] value has a constant value, this is a *linear* equation. The slope would be the difference in [`y`]-values or [$a].  The value of [`b`] would be the value at [`x=0`] or [$b]. Thus the equation is 
[``` y= [$f]```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
